### Description

This Terraform program configures a Route 53 hosted zone named "main" and sets up weighted routing policies to distribute traffic between a primary database instance and two replica database instances. The primary and replica instances are represented as DNS records within the "main" zone.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "main_zone" {
  name = "main.example.com"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary_record" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.main_zone.zone_id
  name    = "primary"
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = 300

  weighted_routing_policy {
    weight = 100
  }

  records = ["192.168.1.1"]  # Replace with actual primary DB instance IP
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "replica_1_record" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.main_zone.zone_id
  name    = "replica-1"
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = 300

  weighted_routing_policy {
    weight = 45
  }

  records = ["192.168.1.2"]  # Replace with actual replica-1 DB instance IP
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "replica_2_record" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.main_zone.zone_id
  name    = "replica-2"
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = 300

  weighted_routing_policy {
    weight = 45
  }

  records = ["192.168.1.3"]  # Replace with actual replica-2 DB instance IP
}
```

### Explanation

1. **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured to use the `us-west-2` region.
2. **Route 53 Hosted Zone**: A Route 53 hosted zone named "main.example.com" is created.
3. **Primary Record**: An A record named "primary" is created within the "main" zone. It uses a weighted routing policy with a weight of 100.
4. **Replica Records**: Two A records named "replica-1" and "replica-2" are created within the "main" zone. Each uses a weighted routing policy with a weight of 45.

Replace the placeholder IP addresses (`192.168.1.1`, `192.168.1.2`, `192.168.1.3`) with the actual IP addresses of your database instances.

