### Description
This Terraform program sets up a blue/green deployment using Route 53 to direct traffic to two separate environments (`blue` and `green`) that share a common RDS database (`myapp_db`). The program includes the necessary IAM roles and configurations to ensure smooth operation and failover capabilities.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

# Create an IAM role for EC2 instances
resource "aws_iam_role" "eb_ec2_role" {
  name = "eb_ec2_role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Attach policies to the IAM role
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "ec2_amazon_rds_full_access" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonRDSFullAccess"
  role       = aws_iam_role.eb_ec2_role.name
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "ec2_amazon_s3_full_access" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonS3FullAccess"
  role       = aws_iam_role.eb_ec2_role.name
}

# Create an instance profile for the IAM role
resource "aws_iam_instance_profile" "eb_ec2_profile" {
  name = "eb_ec2_profile"
  roles = [aws_iam_role.eb_ec2_role.name]
}

# Create the RDS database
resource "aws_db_instance" "myapp_db" {
  instance_class = "db.t2.micro"
  allocated_storage = 20
  engine = "mysql"
  username = "admin"
  password = "password"
  skip_final_snapshot = true
}

# Create a Route 53 hosted zone
resource "aws_route53_zone" "myapp_zone" {
  name = "example.com"
}

# Create a health check for the blue environment
resource "aws_route53_health_check" "blue_health_check" {
  fqdn              = "blue.example.com"
  port              = 80
  type              = "HTTP"
  resource_path     = "/"
  failure_threshold = 3
  request_interval  = 30
}

# Create a health check for the green environment
resource "aws_route53_health_check" "green_health_check" {
  fqdn              = "green.example.com"
  port              = 80
  type              = "HTTP"
  resource_path     = "/"
  failure_threshold = 3
  request_interval  = 30
}

# Create a Route 53 record for the blue environment
resource "aws_route53_record" "blue_record" {
  name    = "blue"
  type    = "A"
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.myapp_zone.zone_id
  ttl     = 300

  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "PRIMARY"
  }

  health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.blue_health_check.id

  records = ["192.168.1.1"]  # Replace with actual IP address
}

# Create a Route 53 record for the green environment
resource "aws_route53_record" "green_record" {
  name    = "green"
  type    = "A"
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.myapp_zone.zone_id
  ttl     = 300

  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "SECONDARY"
  }

  health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.green_health_check.id

  records = ["192.168.1.2"]  # Replace with actual IP address
}
```

### Explanation
- **IAM Role and Instance Profile**: An IAM role (`eb_ec2_role`) is created with necessary permissions, and an instance profile (`eb_ec2_profile`) is attached to it.
- **RDS Database**: A MySQL RDS instance (`myapp_db`) is created with basic configurations.
- **Route 53 Hosted Zone**: A Route 53 hosted zone (`myapp_zone`) is created for managing DNS records.
- **Health Checks**: Health checks are set up for both the blue and green environments to monitor their availability.
- **Route 53 Records**: Two A records (`blue_record` and `green_record`) are created with failover routing policies to direct traffic based on the health status of the environments.

