### Description
The Terraform program below configures a Route 53 zone named "primary" and sets up a routing policy that directs users to the least congested endpoint between two regions, "us-east-1" and "eu-central-1". The routing policy uses the `latency_routing_policy` to achieve this.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "primary" {
  name = "example.com"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary_record" {
  name       = "www"
  type       = "A"
  zone_id    = aws_route53_zone.primary.zone_id
  ttl        = 300

  latency_routing_policy {
    region = "us-east-1"
  }

  records = ["192.0.2.1"]  # Example IP address for US East region

  depends_on = [aws_route53_record.primary_record_eu]
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary_record_eu" {
  name       = "www"
  type       = "A"
  zone_id    = aws_route53_zone.primary.zone_id
  ttl        = 300

  latency_routing_policy {
    region = "eu-central-1"
  }

  records = ["198.51.100.1"]  # Example IP address for EU Central region
}
```

### Explanation
1. **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured to use the "us-east-1" region.
2. **Route 53 Zone**: A Route 53 zone named "example.com" is created.
3. **Route 53 Record for US East Region**: An A record named "www" is created with a latency routing policy targeting the "us-east-1" region. The record points to an example IP address "192.0.2.1".
4. **Route 53 Record for EU Central Region**: Another A record named "www" is created with a latency routing policy targeting the "eu-central-1" region. The record points to an example IP address "198.51.100.1".

This setup ensures that users are directed to the least congested endpoint based on their geographic location.

