### Description
The Terraform program below creates an EC2 instance using the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI. The program includes a provider configuration for AWS, specifying the region. It also defines an IAM role and attaches the necessary policies to allow the EC2 instance to perform basic operations. The EC2 instance is configured with a default instance type and other necessary attributes.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

# Create an IAM role for the EC2 instance
resource "aws_iam_role" "ec2_instance_role" {
  name = "ec2-instance-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Attach the AmazonEC2ReadOnlyAccess policy to the IAM role
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "ec2_read_only_access" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonEC2ReadOnlyAccess"
  role       = aws_iam_role.ec2_instance_role.name
}

# Create the EC2 instance using the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI
resource "aws_instance" "latest_amazon_linux_2_instance" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"  # Example AMI ID for Amazon Linux 2, replace with actual latest AMI ID
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
  iam_instance_profile = aws_iam_role.ec2_instance_role.name

  tags = {
    Name = "LatestAmazonLinux2Instance"
  }
}
```

### Notes
- The `ami` attribute in the `aws_instance` resource is set to an example AMI ID for Amazon Linux 2. You should replace `"ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"` with the actual latest AMI ID for Amazon Linux 2 in your desired region.
- The IAM role `ec2-instance-role` is created with a trust policy that allows EC2 to assume the role.
- The `AmazonEC2ReadOnlyAccess` policy is attached to the IAM role to provide read-only access to EC2 resources.
- The EC2 instance is launched with the `t3.micro` instance type and tagged for easy identification.

