### Description
This Terraform program creates an Application Load Balancer (ALB) with an Authenticate-OIDC action. The ALB listens on port 443 and uses OIDC for authentication. The necessary OIDC parameters such as authorization endpoint, client ID, client secret, issuer, token endpoint, and user info endpoint need to be provided.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_lb" "my_load_balancer" {
  name               = "my-load-balancer"
  internal           = false
  load_balancer_type = "application"
  subnets            = ["subnet-0123456789abcdef0", "subnet-0fedcba9876543210"]
  security_groups    = ["sg-0123456789abcdef0"]

  access_logs {
    bucket = "my-alb-access-logs"
    prefix = "logs/"
  }
}

resource "aws_lb_listener" "my_load_balancer_listener" {
  load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.my_load_balancer.arn
  port              = 443
  protocol          = "HTTPS"

  default_action {
    type = "authenticate-oidc"

    authenticate_oidc {
      authorization_endpoint = "https://example.com/auth"
      client_id              = "client-id"
      client_secret          = "client-secret"
      issuer                 = "https://example.com"
      token_endpoint         = "https://example.com/token"
      user_info_endpoint     = "https://example.com/userinfo"
    }
  }
}
```

### Notes
- Replace the placeholder values for `authorization_endpoint`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `issuer`, `token_endpoint`, and `user_info_endpoint` with your actual OIDC provider details.
- Ensure that the subnets and security groups specified in the `aws_lb` resource exist in your AWS account and are correctly configured.
- The `access_logs` block specifies an S3 bucket where the access logs will be stored. Make sure the bucket exists and the ALB has the necessary permissions to write to it.

